# Install Obot Sentry on Windows

Install Obot Sentry on a Windows device without an MDM. This install package
contains:

- `obot-sentry.msi` — the installer (version {{.obotSentryVersion}}). Installs
  the agent and schedules scans and hook convergence, exactly as the MDM
  channel does.
- `obot-sentry.exe` — the bare binary, for installing by hand instead.

Everything below needs an Administrator PowerShell.

## Installer (MSI)

1. Install the agent, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your
   enrollment key:

   ```powershell
   msiexec /i "obot-sentry.msi" /qn SERVER_URL="{{.serverURL}}" ENROLLMENT_KEY="REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY" SCAN_INTERVAL_MINUTES="{{.scanIntervalMinutes}}"{{if .enforcementEnabled}} ENFORCEMENT=1{{end}}
   ```
{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
   `ENFORCEMENT=1` writes `EnforcementEnabled` to the registry, which the hook
   task reads. Keep it in the command you use for upgrades too: an install
   command without it writes 0 and stops enforcing.
{{end}}
2. Trigger the first scan without waiting for the schedule (optional):

   ```powershell
   Start-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'Obot Sentry Scan'
   ```

## What the installer does

- Installs `obot-sentry.exe` under `C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry`, next
  to `obot-sentryw.exe` — a small launcher the scan task uses so scheduled
  scans never flash a console window on screen.
- Writes the deployment configuration from the install command's properties to
  `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry`.
- Registers a machine-wide scheduled task (`Obot Sentry Scan`) that runs in each
  signed-in user's session, as that user, at logon and every 10 minutes. Each
  run submits a scan at most every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes — change
  `ScanIntervalMinutes` in the registry without reinstalling.
- Registers a second machine-wide scheduled task (`Obot Sentry Hook Install`)
  running as SYSTEM at logon and hourly, converging the managed local-agent
  hooks for the active console user. It reads `EnforcementEnabled` from the
  registry on every run, so turning enforcement on or off there takes effect
  without reinstalling.
- Handles upgrades and uninstalls out of the box.

## Verify the install

1. Ensure the agent was installed:

   ```powershell
   & "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" version
   ```

   This prints the installed version.

2. Ensure the settings are readable:

   ```powershell
   Get-ItemProperty 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry'
   ```

   If configured correctly, this should display the values from step 1.

3. Ensure both scheduled tasks are registered:

   ```powershell
   Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'Obot Sentry Scan','Obot Sentry Hook Install' | Get-ScheduledTaskInfo
   ```

   This will produce the following output:

   - `LastRunTime` climbs on schedule — every 10 minutes for the scan task,
     hourly for the hook task, and at each logon for both.
   - `LastTaskResult = 0` means the last run succeeded. Non-zero is usually a
     configuration problem — recheck the registry values from step 2. `267011`
     means the task has not run yet.
   - `NumberOfMissedRuns` above 0 means the device was asleep or off when a run
     was due; the next run catches up.

4. Ensure scans are landing:

   To get the results of the latest scan attempt, run the following command:

   ```powershell
   Get-Content "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\obot\obot-sentry\scan.json"
   ```

   `lastSubmitAt` records the last successful submission, and `lastStatus` is `ok` or
   `error` with `lastError` carrying the reason.

   The adjacent `scan-logs` directory retains up to 14 days of the most recent scan attempts.

## Standalone binary (Alternate)

Install `obot-sentry.exe` by hand to run one-off scans or install hooks
yourself.

1. Install the binary:

   ```powershell
   New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry" | Out-Null
   Copy-Item obot-sentry.exe "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe"
   ```

2. Configure Obot Sentry, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your
   enrollment key:

   ```powershell
   $key = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry'
   New-Item -Path $key -Force | Out-Null
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name ServerURL -Value "{{.serverURL}}"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name EnrollmentKey -Value "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name ScanIntervalMinutes -Value "{{.scanIntervalMinutes}}"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name EnforcementEnabled -Type DWord -Value {{if .enforcementEnabled}}1{{else}}0{{end}}
   ```

3. Enroll the device:

   ```powershell
   & "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" enroll
   ```

   Enroll verifies the configuration and prints the device ID this machine
   reports as. It is idempotent, so running it again is harmless.

### Run a scan

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" scan --submit --force
```

Scans are submitted at most once every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes, and a
run within that window skips submission. `--force` bypasses the interval,
causing the scan to be submitted immediately. The same command works after an MSI
install.

### Install hooks

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" hook-install{{if .enforcementEnabled}} --enforce{{end}}
```
{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
## Tool-call enforcement

Each tool call in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor is checked against Obot's
allowlist before it runs. The check is a single request to Obot, and it is
**fail-closed**: if Obot cannot be reached, the device is not enrolled, or the
hook cannot tell which MCP server a call targets, the call is blocked.

- Blocking a call never *approves* one. Your agents' own approval prompts still
  apply to everything that is allowed.
- Visual Studio Code is not supported for enforcement. Its audit hook is unaffected.
- An MCP server can be allowlisted by URL, by its `npx`/`uvx` package, or — for a
  claude.ai connector — by display name. A stdio server started from a local
  executable path presents none of those, so it cannot be allowlisted and is
  blocked by design.

Turning enforcement off in Obot stops the blocking immediately — the server
allows every call when enforcement is disabled — even before the hooks
themselves are removed. To turn it off on this device instead, set the registry
value and let the hook task reconverge:

```powershell
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry' -Name EnforcementEnabled -Type DWord -Value 0
```
{{end}}
## Uninstall

Remove the hooks first, then Obot Sentry itself. Uninstalling the MSI
unregisters both scheduled tasks but leaves hook configuration in place, so the
order matters.

1. Remove the Obot Sentry-managed hooks, preserving third-party hooks:

   ```powershell
   & "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" hook-uninstall
   ```

2. If you installed the MSI, uninstall it — this also unregisters the scheduled
   tasks, so no further scans or hook convergence happen:

   ```powershell
   msiexec /x "obot-sentry.msi" /qn
   ```

   If you installed the standalone binary, remove the binary, the configuration,
   and the device identity and scan state:

   ```powershell
   Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
   Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
   Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:ProgramData\obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
   Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
   ```

Hook removal preserves third-party hooks and only touches entries marked
`--managed-by obot-sentry`; it leaves unmarked supporting settings alone. Remove
those by hand if you want them gone: the Codex `[features]` pins `hooks = true`
and `non_prefixed_mcp_tool_names = false`, and the Obot-added values under VS
Code's `chat.hookFilesLocations`.

Restart Claude Code, Codex, VS Code, and Cursor afterward to drop the hooks.
